WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.152 --> 00:00:16.322
My grabbed the poker with Oliver's shoe and pulled it out of the fire.

2
00:00:17.322 --> 00:00:20.685
The shoe was no longer in flames, but it was still smoking.

3
00:00:22.506 --> 00:00:25.688
My mom hurried to the kitchen sink and ran water over the shoe.

4
00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:30.886
Now Oliver's shoe was burned and wet.

5
00:00:33.608 --> 00:00:35.409
Oliver started yelling.

6
00:00:35.429 --> 00:00:37.631
"Iggy ruined my shoe!"

7
00:00:37.651 --> 00:00:40.813
"Wow, Iggy," Tate murmured.

8
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:42.774
"You really messed up."

9
00:00:42.794 --> 00:00:47.697
"I was only trying to invent a shoe warmer," I said.

10
00:00:47.717 --> 00:00:51.180
"Inventors are always thinking of new ideas."

11
00:00:54.764 --> 00:00:59.027
"Well, you're going to have to think in your room for a while," Mom said, frowning.

12
00:00:59.047 --> 00:01:02.508
"And I want you to apologize to Oliver."

13
00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:06.131
"Sorry," I mumbled.

14
00:01:07.191 --> 00:01:09.893
Oliver just stared down at his ruined shoe.

15
00:01:11.073 --> 00:01:14.595
In my room I thought about what a bad attitude everyone had.

16
00:01:15.616 --> 00:01:16.496
Well, not everyone.

17
00:01:16.797 --> 00:01:18.297
But Tate had a bad attitude.

18
00:01:19.318 --> 00:01:20.879
He didn't have to say I messed up.

19
00:01:21.959 --> 00:01:22.580
And Oliver.

20
00:01:22.996 --> 00:01:25.937
He had a terrible attitude about visiting us.

21
00:01:27.338 --> 00:01:28.078
I sighed.

22
00:01:28.098 --> 00:01:34.721
"Wouldn't it be great," I thought, "if there was something that could change people's attitudes?"

23
00:01:35.782 --> 00:01:40.924
That's when I got an idea for a new invention—an Amazing Attitude Adjuster!

24
00:01:41.704 --> 00:01:42.885
What a great idea!

25
00:01:44.085 --> 00:01:47.627
With inventions, you have to figure out how they will actually work.

26
00:01:49.527 --> 00:01:50.648
That's the hard part.

27
00:01:51.488 --> 00:01:54.650
That was what had gone wrong with my shoe-warmer invention.

28
00:01:55.691 --> 00:01:57.152
I hadn't thought about it enough.

29
00:01:57.913 --> 00:02:00.174
But this time I was going to think a lot.

30
00:02:01.195 --> 00:02:03.977
I would invent the perfect attitude adjuster!

31
00:02:04.918 --> 00:02:08.400
By the time Mom said I could come out of my room, I felt happy again.

32
00:02:09.621 --> 00:02:14.405
For me, an idea for a new invention is the best attitude adjuster ever.

33
00:02:16.186 --> 00:02:18.588
Josh, Jenny, and Molly had gone home.

34
00:02:20.224 --> 00:02:22.386
Tate was doing his homework on the computer.

35
00:02:23.487 --> 00:02:27.269
Oliver was walking around the apartment with his burned shoe half on.

36
00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:31.933
It had shrunk in the fire, and now it didn't fit on his foot.

37
00:02:31.953 --> 00:02:35.476
"My foot hurts in this shoe!"

38
00:02:36.076 --> 00:02:38.378
he complained, stomping around.

39
00:02:38.398 --> 00:02:41.360
"Well, why don't you take it off?"

40
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:42.341
I asked.

41
00:02:42.361 --> 00:02:46.444
"I can't take it off," said Oliver.

42
00:02:47.612 --> 00:02:50.913
"I might get a splinter in my foot from these wood floors."

43
00:02:53.494 --> 00:02:55.095
I let out a loud sigh.

44
00:02:56.296 --> 00:02:59.937
Now Oliver was complaining about things that hadn't even happened yet!

45
00:03:01.058 --> 00:03:01.858
Tate looked up.

46
00:03:02.718 --> 00:03:05.079
"Why did the boy bury his flashlight?"

47
00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:07.240
he asked Oliver, winking at me.

48
00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.361
"I don't know," said Oliver.

49
00:03:10.381 --> 00:03:16.624
"Why?" "Because," said Tate, "the batteries were dead."

50
00:03:18.036 --> 00:03:19.197
I started laughing.

51
00:03:19.217 --> 00:03:22.620
"Ha-ha! Get it?

52
00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:23.901
Oliver?" I said.

53
00:03:23.921 --> 00:03:29.885
"The flashlight's batteries were dead so the boy buried the flashlight!"

54
00:03:31.146 --> 00:03:32.387
Oliver stopped stomping.

55
00:03:32.407 --> 00:03:34.288
"That's funny!"

56
00:03:34.828 --> 00:03:35.829
he said, grinning.

57
00:03:35.849 --> 00:03:37.711
"Tell me another joke."

58
00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:42.974
Tate told Oliver another joke, and Oliver burst out laughing.

59
00:03:46.576 --> 00:03:49.799
When I went into the kitchen, Mom greeted me with a smile.

60
00:03:49.819 --> 00:03:53.062
"Tate's joke seemed to cheer up Oliver."

61
00:03:53.082 --> 00:03:58.566
I remembered something my grandfather told me once.

62
00:03:59.527 --> 00:04:03.170
He said when he was growing up, there was a service called Dial-a-Joke.

63
00:04:04.171 --> 00:04:06.593
You could call a special number on the telephone.

64
00:04:07.493 --> 00:04:09.855
A recording would come on and tell you a joke.

65
00:04:11.016 --> 00:04:15.160
My grandfather said that calling Dial-a-Joke always put him in a great mood.

66
00:04:16.212 --> 00:04:17.954
"Hmm I thought.

67
00:04:18.814 --> 00:04:21.757
I was pretty sure that Molly had an old toy telephone.

68
00:04:22.658 --> 00:04:24.940
It had an old-fashioned wheel for dialing.

69
00:04:26.601 --> 00:04:30.945
If I could use it to tell jokes, I could change people's attitudes.

70
00:04:31.985 --> 00:04:34.247
I could make them laugh and feel happy!

71
00:04:34.267 --> 00:04:36.049
"Wow, Iggy!"

72
00:04:36.429 --> 00:04:37.130
I told myself.

73
00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:39.752
"That is a fantastic idea!"

74
00:04:41.173 --> 00:04:44.436
It was more than that—it was an award-winning idea.

75
00:04:45.489 --> 00:04:48.413
A "get into the Inventor's Hall of Fame" idea!

76
00:04:50.396 --> 00:04:52.980
And cranky Oliver had helped me think of it!
